# openplate needs NO configuration to boot. There is no database, no session
# secret, no encryption key and no account system — the server stores nothing.
# Everything below is optional tuning.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Public URL this instance is reachable at (e.g. used when building absolute
# links). REQUIRED in production — the server refuses to boot without it.
# Default: http://localhost:3000.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# UI language a visitor sees BEFORE they have chosen one. `en` or `de`.
# Default: en. The locale cookie always wins over this, so it picks a starting
# language, never a locked one — anyone can still switch in Settings.
# An unsupported code fails the boot rather than falling back, so a typo is
# loud instead of serving the wrong language forever.
# This is the UI only: it does not translate food names or AI replies.
# DEFAULT_UI_LANGUAGE=de

# Dev server — comma-separated hostnames Vite should allow in addition to the
# built-in defaults (e.g. your tailnet MagicDNS name). See vite.config.ts.
# VITE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=my-machine,my-machine.tailnet.ts.net

# Reverse-proxy trust — controls Express `trust proxy`, so req.protocol/hostname/ip
# and the URL the @react-router/express adapter builds reflect X-Forwarded-* headers.
# REQUIRED behind a proxy in React Router v8: the CSRF check matches the browser
# Origin against request.url's host, so same-origin actions are aborted if this is off.
# It also decides which X-Forwarded-For entry the food-lookup rate limiter buckets on.
# Values: hop count (1 = single Traefik hop), true/false, or a CIDR/preset list.
# Default: 1 in production, off in development. Multi-proxy (e.g. Cloudflare -> Traefik) = 2.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# LowCarbCheck food database integration.
# openplate queries the public LowCarbCheck food database to attach curated
# nutrition data + food images to the foods it identifies in your plate photos.
# Only the food NAMES are sent (e.g. "grilled chicken breast") — never the
# photos, and never anything else about you. The lookup is fail-open: if the
# service is unreachable or disabled, plate identification still works exactly
# as before (using only your AI provider's estimates).
# Default: https://lowcarbcheck.org. Set to an EMPTY string to disable entirely
# (no food names ever leave your machine).
# FOOD_DB_API_URL=https://lowcarbcheck.org

# Optional end-to-end-encrypted sync (M128). Point this at a running
# openplate-sync service (https://github.com/…/openplate-sync, or the hosted
# one) and the app grows an opt-in "Sync" screen: create an account, choose a
# master passphrase, and your diary is encrypted ON YOUR DEVICE before it is
# uploaded. The server stores opaque ciphertext — it has no key and cannot
# decrypt anything, by construction rather than by policy.
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET (the default) and openplate is a purely local app: no sync UI
# renders anywhere and not a single sync request leaves your browser.
#
# Plate photos always stay on the device — they are never part of a sync blob.
#
# The origin of this URL is appended to the production CSP `connect-src`
# automatically; you do NOT need to add it to CSP_CONNECT_EXTRA below.
# A malformed value fails the boot deliberately, so a typo can't look like
# "sync is quietly off".
# SYNC_SERVER_URL=https://sync.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instance-provided AI (optional) — one-click "use this openplate's AI"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If you run an OpenAI-compatible vision endpoint next to openplate (e.g. an
# `openplate-inference` container in the same compose file, or Ollama/vLLM/
# LM Studio on the same box), point these at it. Every browser using this
# instance then gets a one-tap "This openplate provides its own AI" connect on
# the AI settings page and the scan screen, instead of each person having to go
# and get their own provider key.
#
# LEAVE THEM UNSET (the default) and nothing changes: no card renders anywhere,
# nothing extra is sent to the browser, and bring-your-own-key is the only path.
# A malformed DEFAULT_INFERENCE_BASE_URL fails the boot deliberately, so a typo
# can't look like "the button just never appeared".
#
# Must be an address a BROWSER can reach — the photo goes from the device
# straight to this endpoint, never through the openplate server. A container
# hostname like `http://openplate-inference:8080/v1` therefore does NOT work;
# publish the endpoint (or put it behind your reverse proxy) and use that URL.
# Its origin is added to the production CSP `connect-src` automatically, so you
# do NOT also need CSP_CONNECT_EXTRA below.
#
# ⚠ SECURITY — READ BEFORE SETTING DEFAULT_INFERENCE_API_KEY
# This key is NOT kept on the server. It is sent to every browser that loads
# this instance, embedded in the page HTML — anyone who can open the app can
# read it with view-source. That is fine for an endpoint only your household or
# your network can reach; it is NOT fine for a metered cloud provider key, and
# it is NOT fine on an instance exposed to the open internet without another
# access boundary (VPN, tailnet, auth proxy) in front of it. If your endpoint
# needs no key at all, leave this unset — that is the common local case.
# DEFAULT_INFERENCE_BASE_URL=https://ai.example.com/v1
# DEFAULT_INFERENCE_API_KEY=
# DEFAULT_INFERENCE_MODEL=openplate-plate-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Newsletter capture on the landing page (optional) — OFF unless you set BOTH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# openplate ships with no mailing list. A list belongs to whoever runs an
# instance, so LEAVING THESE UNSET (the default, and the self-host default)
# means the feature does not exist here: no sign-up section renders on the
# landing page, the landing route accepts no POST, no third-party script is
# loaded, and the production CSP is byte-for-byte what it was without it.
#
# Set both and the landing page grows a sign-up section that posts to the
# openplate SERVER, which forwards it to NEWSLETTER_SUBSCRIBE_URL. The browser
# never learns that address — it is your infrastructure, not the visitor's
# business — so the endpoint may live on a private network your app server can
# reach and nobody else can.
#
# BOTH OR NEITHER. NEWSLETTER_SUBSCRIBE_URL on its own would ship a bare email
# box with no bot protection in front of a personal-data form, so a
# half-configured pair fails the boot deliberately.
#
# The endpoint receives JSON: {email, locale, consent, source, turnstileToken}.
# NEWSLETTER_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY is a Cloudflare Turnstile SITE key (public by
# design — it is rendered into the widget). The matching SECRET key belongs to
# whatever verifies the token at the endpoint; openplate never sees it.
# NEWSLETTER_SUBSCRIBE_URL=https://newsletter.example.com/api/subscribe
# NEWSLETTER_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x0000000000000000000000

# Extra connect-src origins for the production Content-Security-Policy.
# The BYOK vision call runs browser -> AI provider directly, so the CSP only
# allows 'self', the built-in providers' origins (OpenRouter, Mistral,
# Anthropic — derived from the provider registry), and localhost/127.0.0.1 by
# default — enough for a local openai-compatible endpoint (Ollama, vLLM,
# LM Studio...) on the same box. Set this (space-separated origins) if your
# endpoint is a REMOTE host instead. See docs/configuration.md's
# "Custom AI endpoints" section.
# CSP_CONNECT_EXTRA=https://ai.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYOK provider keys — NOT read by the app. Local tooling only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# openplate never reads a provider API key from the environment. A real user's
# key is entered in the browser, stored on the device
# (app/lib/local-store/ai-settings.ts) and sent browser -> provider directly;
# the server is not in that path and has no copy. Setting these in a deployed
# instance's environment does NOTHING — see "BYOK Security Rules" in AGENTS.md.
#
# They exist purely so a DEVELOPER can point verification scripts at a live
# provider (catalog pricing checks, the structured-output smoke test). Leave
# them empty — or absent entirely — for a normal self-host.
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
